  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nNo real change in the forecast. Ridge flattens out on Thursday as a \nbroad upper trough, and associated weak frontal boundary, advances \nfrom north to south toward our region. Most of Thursday should be \ndry. Then, chances for showers and storms increase Thursday evening \nand overnight, north of I-40, as the boundary draws closer.\n\nFront likely washes out on Friday over the area. However, a weak \nshortwave then moves across for Friday and Saturday. This will keep \nchances of showers and storms in the forecast, mainly during the \nafternoon and evening hours. While strong storm or two are possible, \nno severe weather is expected. Main concern with any stronger storm \nwill be locally heavy rainfall due to elevated PW values of 2 \ninches. \n\nEarly next week, riding builds back in. This will restrict precip \nchances to the high terrain during peak heating. By mid-week, we \nstart to see a more zonal flow again. This will open the door for \nslightly better coverage of precip chances across the area. Late \nnext week, another broad trough and frontal boundary approach from \nthe north. But it will be a battle between his feature and the high \nto our southeast. Hopefully the high breaks down and we can get \nsome additional rain chances across the area. \n\nTemperatures remain above average through the period. If CHA hits 90 \ntoday, it will be day 37 of consecutive days at or above 90 \ndegrees. The all time record is 38 days set back in 1993. The \ncurrent forecast calls for above 90 degree temps for CHA for the \nnext 7 days. They are likely to hit it tomorrow too, but the \nstreak could be disrupted if CHA sees showers and storms on either\nFriday/Saturday.",
